Abstract

This dissertation establishes the mathematical foundations of a new subfield: Thermodynamic Multi-Agent Intelligence Systems (TMAIS). The central thesis is that AGI-class behavior is not a property of any single model but an emergent property of a formally orchestrated ensemble of specialized agents operating on a shared Riemannian semantic manifold, subject to PAC-optimal routing, Riemannian contraction stability, entropy-budget constraints, and certified memory safety under adversarial perturbation.

This formulation is directly supported by two convergent 2026 results: single-model scaling provably hits thermodynamic ceilings derivable from Landauer and Stefan-Boltzmann constraints (arXiv:2602.05463, Takahashi & Hayashi), and closed-loop recursive self-improvement provably collapses via entropy decay and variance amplification unless exogenous signal persists (arXiv:2601.05280, Zenil). TMAIS makes both results operational design constraints rather than post-hoc concerns.

The ensemble architecture comprises five distinct model families, each representing a fundamentally different approach to language intelligence: OpenAI GPT-5.4 (released March 2026) with 1M-token native context, native computer-use capabilities, and a unified reasoning-coding-agentic architecture; Anthropic Claude Opus 4.7 (released April 2026) with xhigh effort reasoning levels, 2576-pixel high-resolution vision, and autonomous coding capabilities verified by internal consistency checks; Google Gemini 2.0 (released early 2026) with native full-modal embedding architecture integrating text, images, audio, video, and PDFs into a unified vector space; xAI Grok-3 (released February 2025) with 3T-parameter Mixture-of-Experts architecture, deep domain knowledge in finance, healthcare, law, and science, trained on the 200K-GPU Colossus cluster; and Meta Llama 4 (Maverick and Scout variants, released 2025) with MoE architecture (up to 400B total parameters, 128 experts) and native multimodal processing across 200 languages.

The USA AGI Gigafactory — a nationally distributed, nuclear-powered, closed-loop intelligence production system orchestrating these five LLM ecosystems — is the instantiation of this theory, not its core. The core is the theory: a mathematically rigorous treatment of how heterogeneous specialized models are composed into a system that is provably complete, optimally routed, geometrically stable, thermodynamically accountable, and recursively improvable without collapse.

Six original contributions are defended with complete mathematical proofs. Lemma 0 establishes distribution-relative, shift-robust ε-semantic completeness for the heterogeneous ensemble. MROT-S proves PAC-optimal routing from finite data via Bernstein concentration, with specialist sample-complexity reduction. CVF-R proves exponential convergence of multi-model validation on a curvature-bounded Riemannian manifold, providing the first explicit coupling between cross-model alignment quality and contraction rate. CTB-L operationalizes the Landauer energy floor via a telemetry-computable log-likelihood proxy — realizing the bits-per-joule framework of arXiv:2602.05463 — and introduces the Maakoul Efficiency Coefficient η_M as a universal thermodynamic routing metric. Memory ISS certifies memory safety under bounded hallucination injection via Volterra Lyapunov construction and the Small-Gain Theorem. Relaxed SRI provides a three-gate probabilistic stability architecture for ensemble recursive self-improvement deployable at practical inference speeds. Together these constitute a complete certification chain, a new research program, and the physics-first answer to scaling AGI beyond single-model thermodynamic limits.

