1. Introduction and Motivation

1.1 Three Convergent Crises

The model-centric paradigm — scaling a single model in parameters and data toward general intelligence — faces simultaneous pressure from three independent directions, each now supported by formal 2026 results.

The thermodynamic wall. Takahashi and Hayashi (arXiv:2602.05463, February 2026) derive Landauer-scale closed-cycle benchmarks for intelligence acquisition: bits-per-joule metrics — Thermodynamic Epiplexity per Joule and Empowerment per Joule — under explicit boundary and closure assumptions. They demonstrate that modern AI systems achieve remarkable capabilities at the cost of substantial energy consumption, and that the gap between biological intelligence (∼20 W human brain) and current AI systems is orders of magnitude. Combined with Stefan-Boltzmann radiative equilibrium constraints, recent analyses derive a hard sustainable computation ceiling approximately equivalent to GPT-9 under current energy infrastructure. The model-centric paradigm is scaling toward a physical boundary it cannot cross. TMAIS is architecturally positioned beyond that boundary, because it extends the intelligence frontier by composing existing models rather than training ever-larger ones.

The specialization-generalization impossibility. A model trained to minimize loss across a heterogeneous task distribution faces conflicting gradient pressures. No single parameter configuration minimizes loss uniformly across all task types. This is not a calibration problem — it is a structural mathematical impossibility. Recent empirical work on heterogeneous multi-LLM systems confirms substantial performance gains from cross-model specialization over single-model deployment. The X-MAS framework demonstrated up to 47% performance improvement on the AIME dataset when transitioning from homogeneous to heterogeneous LLM-driven multi-agent systems. The gap is structural, not calibrational.

The recursive self-improvement collapse. Zenil (arXiv:2601.05280, January–February 2026) formalizes closed-loop self-training as a discrete-time dynamical system and proves that as training data becomes increasingly self-generated — the parameter $\alpha_t \to 0$ — the system undergoes provably degenerative dynamics: entropy decay, where finite sampling effects induce monotonic loss of distributional diversity, and variance amplification, where the absence of persistent grounding causes distributional drift via a random-walk mechanism. This is the formal result that makes unconstrained recursive self-improvement unsafe at scale. The Relaxed SRI architecture of this dissertation is the direct engineered response: it enforces persistent exogenous anchoring and ensemble disagreement bounds to prevent the degenerative regime.

1.2 The System-Centric Alternative

This dissertation establishes the formal foundations of the alternative paradigm. Intelligence is an emergent property of an orchestrated system, not a monolithic model. The central definition is:

\text{AGI} := \lim_{t \to \infty} \mathcal{O}_t\!\bigl(\{M_i\}_{i=1}^N,\, \mathcal{R}_t,\, \mathcal{F}_t,\, \mathcal{V}_t\bigr)

subject to the constraint system:

