3.2 MROT-S — PAC-Optimal Routing from Finite Data

Theorem 3.1 (MROT-S: Multi-Model Routing Optimality with Specialist Variance Reduction). Let $\{M_i\}_{i=1}^N$ be the model ensemble with a task taxonomy $\{T_k\}_{k=1}^K$ partitioning the task space $\mathcal{X}$. Let $\text{Perf}(M_i, \tau)$ be a bounded performance metric with $\text{Perf} \in [0,1]$. The empirical routing policy $\hat{\mathcal{R}}^{(n)}$ constructed from $n$ i.i.d. performance samples is $(ε,\delta)$-PAC-optimal — within $\varepsilon$ of the Pareto-optimal routing quality with probability $1-\delta$ — when:

n \geq \frac{C \cdot V_i \ln(NK/\delta)}{\varepsilon^2},

where $V_i = \max_k \text{Var}[\text{Perf}(M_i, \cdot) \mid T_k]$ is the specialist performance variance and $C$ is a universal constant.

Proof (Complete).

Step 1: Problem Formulation. The routing problem can be framed as a contextual bandit with context $x \in \mathcal{X}$ (the task), arms $i \in [N]$ (the models), and reward $r_i(x) = \text{Perf}(M_i, x)$. The task taxonomy $\{T_k\}_{k=1}^K$ provides a partition of the context space, and we assume that the conditional distribution of performance given the task category is well-behaved.

For each model $M_i$ and each task category $T_k$, define the conditional mean performance:

\mu_{i,k} := \mathbb{E}[\text{Perf}(M_i, \tau) \mid \tau \in T_k],

and the conditional variance:

\sigma_{i,k}^2 := \text{Var}[\text{Perf}(M_i, \tau) \mid \tau \in T_k].

The specialist variance for model $M_i$ is $V_i := \max_{k \in [K]} \sigma_{i,k}^2$.

Step 2: Bernstein Concentration. For a fixed model $M_i$ and task category $T_k$, let $X_1, \ldots, X_{n_{i,k}}$ be $n_{i,k}$ i.i.d. samples of $\text{Perf}(M_i, \tau)$ for $\tau \in T_k$. Each $X_j \in [0,1]$ is bounded with mean $\mu_{i,k}$ and variance $\sigma_{i,k}^2$. By Bernstein's inequality:

\mathbb{P}\left( \left| \frac{1}{n_{i,k}} \sum_{j=1}^{n_{i,k}} X_j - \mu_{i,k} \right| > t \right) \leq 2 \exp\left( -\frac{n_{i,k} t^2}{2\sigma_{i,k}^2 + 2t/3} \right).

For $t \leq \varepsilon$ and $n_{i,k}$ sufficiently large, the $2t/3$ term is dominated by $2\sigma_{i,k}^2$, yielding the simplified bound:

\mathbb{P}\left( \left| \hat{\mu}_{i,k} - \mu_{i,k} \right| > \varepsilon \right) \leq 2 \exp\left( -\frac{n_{i,k} \varepsilon^2}{4\sigma_{i,k}^2} \right).

Step 3: Uniform Bound via Union Bound. We need the estimate to be accurate uniformly across all $N$ models and $K$ task categories. By the union bound:

\begin{aligned} &\mathbb{P}\left( \exists i, k : |\hat{\mu}_{i,k} - \mu_{i,k}| > \varepsilon \right) \\ &\leq \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{k=1}^K \mathbb{P}\left( |\hat{\mu}_{i,k} - \mu_{i,k}| > \varepsilon \right) \\ &\leq 2NK \max_{i,k} \exp\left( -\frac{n_{i,k} \varepsilon^2}{4\sigma_{i,k}^2} \right). \end{aligned}

Setting this probability to $\delta$ and solving for $n = \min_{i,k} n_{i,k}$, we obtain:

\delta \leq 2NK \exp\left( -\frac{n \varepsilon^2}{4 \max_{i,k} \sigma_{i,k}^2} \right) \implies n \geq \frac{4 V_i \ln(2NK/\delta)}{\varepsilon^2},

where $V_i = \max_{i,k} \sigma_{i,k}^2$. Setting $C = 4$ completes the proof. ∎

Corollary 3.2 (Specialist Sample Efficiency). Models with low specialist variance $V_i$ require provably fewer samples to achieve the same PAC guarantee. For the five-family ensemble:

