4. The Thermodynamic Drag Tensor and SRI

4.1 TDF — Entropy-Budgeted Scaling

Definition 4.1 (Thermodynamic Drag Tensor). The Thermodynamic Drag Tensor (TDF) extends the single-model inequality $\Delta I \leq Q_{\text{diss}} / (k_B T \ln 2)$ to the multi-agent, multi-layer ensemble. For a system with $N$ models and $L$ training layers, the TDF is a $(N \times L)$-dimensional tensor $\mathbf{D} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times L}_{\geq 0}$ whose components $D_{i,\ell}$ represent the entropy production rate per training cycle for model $M_i$ at layer $\ell$:

D_{i,\ell} = \frac{dS_{i,\ell}}{dt} \geq \frac{k_B \ln 2 \cdot \Delta I_{i,\ell}}{T},

where $S_{i,\ell}$ is the entropy and $\Delta I_{i,\ell}$ is the information gain. The tensor components $D_{i,\ell}$ are the operational realization of the epiplexity-per-joule and empowerment-per-joule metrics of arXiv:2602.05463 under closed-cycle boundary assumptions.

Theorem 4.2 (TDF Bounds Collective Intelligence Gain). For the ensemble, the total information gain $\Delta I_{\text{total}}$ is bounded by the total entropy production:

\Delta I_{\text{total}} \leq \frac{1}{k_B T \ln 2} \sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{\ell=1}^L \int_{0}^{t_{\text{cycle}}} D_{i,\ell}(t) dt.

Proof. Summing the individual Landauer bounds over all models and layers yields the collective bound. The inequality is tight when all computations are thermodynamically reversible. ∎

Definition 4.3 (TDF Scheduler). The TDF scheduler enforces a hard entropy budget per training cycle:

\sum_{i=1}^N \sum_{\ell=1}^L \int_{0}^{t_{\text{cycle}}} D_{i,\ell}(t) dt \leq B_{\text{entropy}},

where $B_{\text{entropy}}$ is set by the available cooling capacity and energy infrastructure. A circuit-breaker mechanism suspends training and triggers an entropy audit when the budget is exceeded by a safety margin $\varepsilon_{\text{safe}}$.

4.2 Relaxed SRI — Probabilistic Stability for Ensemble Self-Improvement

Definition 4.4 (Relaxed SRI Constraint Layer). A training cycle at time $t$ is SRI-certified if it passes three probabilistic gates:

Gate 1 — Probabilistic Verification. A lightweight consistency-checking model computes the contradiction probability $p_{\text{contra}}$ for the ensemble output. Certification threshold: $p_{\text{contra}} < p_{\text{cert}}$ (typically $p_{\text{cert}} = 0.05$). This gate directly prevents the logical inconsistency accumulation that drives entropy decay in the Zenil (arXiv:2601.05280) collapse regime.

Gate 2 — Adversarial Consistency. The output embedding $y_{\text{normal}}$ is tested against adversarial prompts. The geodesic distance between outputs is computed: $d_{\text{adv}} = d_{\mathcal{M}}(y_{\text{adv}}, y_{\text{normal}})$. Certification threshold: $d_{\text{adv}} < r_{\text{adv}}$, with $r_{\text{adv}} = \pi/4$. Outputs exceeding this are rejected and flagged.

Gate 3 — Ensemble Disagreement Bound. The cross-model output disagreement is bounded: $\Delta_{\text{agree}} = \max_{i,j} d_{\mathcal{M}}(\Phi_i(y_i), \Phi_j(y_j)) \leq \Delta_{\text{max}}$ (typically $\Delta_{\text{max}} = 0.5$ radians). High disagreement triggers human-in-the-loop review, enforcing the persistent exogenous signal $\alpha_t > 0$ required to prevent the Zenil collapse.

Theorem 4.5 (Probabilistic Stability Guarantee). Under Relaxed SRI with gates $G_1, G_2, G_3$ and anchor set fraction $\alpha_{\min} > 0$, the recursive improvement loop avoids degenerative fixed points with probability at least:

P_{\text{stable}} \geq \prod_{t=1}^{T} (1 - p_{\text{cert}}) \cdot (1 - p_{\text{adv}}) \cdot (1 - p_{\text{disagree}}).

Proof. The Zenil collapse theorem proves that collapse occurs if and only if $\alpha_t \to 0$ and the model trains on its own outputs without external correction. The three gates collectively enforce: (1) Gate 1 prevents internal inconsistency propagation, (2) Gate 2 prevents adversarial drift, (3) Gate 3 enforces $\alpha_t \geq \alpha_{\min} > 0$ by routing high-disagreement tasks to human review. When all three gates pass, the conditions for collapse are violated. ∎

Anchor Set Floor. Every training batch maintains a minimum 25% human-authored anchor set, guaranteeing $\alpha_t \geq 0.25$ even in the worst case.

